19. Synchronize on this

19.1 Definition

When you write

synchronized(this) {

}

🔴 the thread acquires the monitor (intrinsic lock) of the current object (this).
🔴 Only one thread at a time can execute any code synchronized on the same object instance.

19.2 What is this?

Before understanding synchronization, remember what this means.
Example:

class Employee {
    void print() {
        System.out.println(this);
    }
}

Suppose
Employee e1 = new Employee();
Employee e2 = new Employee();

Then
e1.print();
prints Employee@6d06d69c

while
e2.print();
prints Employee@7852e922

Each object has its own this reference.

19.3 Why Synchronize on this?

Suppose an object has shared data.

class Counter {
    private int count;
}

Every thread modifying

count
must coordinate.
So we lock the object itself.

synchronized(this){
    count++;
}

Now only one thread can modify
count at time for that Counter instance.

19.4 Internal Working

Suppose Thread-A enters
counter.increment();

Execution

Acquire Monitor(counter)
↓
Execute
↓
Release Monitor(counter)

Meanwhile Thread-B

Acquire Monitor(counter)
↓
Already Locked
↓
BLOCKED

19.5. Memory Diagram
Heap

+-------------------+
| Counter Object    |
|-------------------|
| count = 5         |
| Monitor           |
+-------------------+

Thread-A
Owns Counter Monitor

Thread-B
BLOCKED

19.6. Why Does It Work?

Because

synchronized(this)
means Lock this object's monitor.

Every object has exactly one monitor.

19.7. Equivalent Code

This

public synchronized void increment(){

}

is equivalent to

public void increment(){
    synchronized(this){

    }
}

This is one of the most asked interview questions.

19.8. Two Objects Example

Suppose

Counter c1 = new Counter();
Counter c2 = new Counter();

Thread-A
c1.increment();

Thread-B
c2.increment();

Question

Will they block each other?

No.
Because

Monitor(c1)
≠
Monitor(c2)

Different objects.
Different monitors.

19.9 Why synchronized(this) Can Be Risky

Example

Library Code

class Counter {
    public void increment(){
        synchronized(this){

        }
    }
}

Application Code

synchronized(counter){
    // Long running operation
}

Now
counter.increment();
is blocked.

The library cannot control who synchronizes on its object.

19.10 Better Design

Instead
private final Object lock = new Object();

Then

synchronized(lock){

}

Now nobody outside the class can accidentally lock your internal monitor.
This is why many production systems prefer private lock objects.


19.11 synchronized(this) vs synchronized(lock)

| Feature                      | `synchronized(this)` | `synchronized(lock)` |
| ---------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------- |
| Lock Object                  | Current object       | Custom lock object   |
| Accessible Outside Class     | ✅ Yes                | ❌ No (if private)    |
| Better Encapsulation         | ❌ No                 | ✅ Yes                |
| Preferred in Enterprise Code | ❌ Usually No         | ✅ Yes                |





